  "full_text": "The fruit is rotting on the vine. Hey, bird. Fuck you. Hey, fuck you. Hey, fuck you, eh? I don't know. Why not? Piece of shit, bird. Fuck you. What are you doing, eh? Hey, you. What? Okay. The fruit is rotting on the vine. Hey, those look like my shoes. Where did those... Those Nikes... Those aren't my shoes. The fuck? That's where... That's where... In the 1980s, she's... Piece of shit came out here, right at the critical time. You know, that's what happened, like, one time, like, I'm masturbating. Phoenix, all of a sudden, the door opens. A door knocked. Piece of shit. Fuck. I hate when that happens. Let go, relax. By wisdom, a house is built. By understanding, it is established. The older I get, the more I appreciate the benefits of taking time. Work. This work done slowly and meticulously by a craftsman is beautiful, able to endure the test of elements, art, whether musical composition, needlework, sculpture, painting, requires time, attention, and detail. Even the cultivation of our walk with God requires a great deal of time to develop. The psalmist realized this when he wrote, Be still and know that I am God. Hubert does not suggest standing around letting your mind wander. No, not that kind of being still. Rather, it means let go, relax. If all this is true of other realms, then responsibility is certainly applicable to the home. Family and children are not created to be jerked up, as my mom used to put it. Be careful with gentleness, attention to detail, require time, lots of it. What if I had kids? I don't have kids. But fuck it, you don't have to fucking be Mr. Freaking Busy Nosey Body Motherfucker. What, man? Be a cool dad? No, no. Fuck my mom and dad. No. You can't masturbate.",
